<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Long-stay Tourists: Resale Planning for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>Every motorbike a long-stay tourist buys in Hanoi carries a second ticket, the resale that must be completed before the boarding pass expires, and the entire art of resale planning is buying the exit at the entrance. This guide writes that art for the visitor specifically, the seller whose deadline is fixed, whose buyer is usually another visitor and whose calendar, not the market, runs the negotiation. It keeps the discipline throughout: no invented prices, no folklore about models, official requirements left with official sources, and the verified rental figures held up as the exit-free alternative.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk has watched seasons of machines and visitors change hands and knows exactly what the last-week seller forgets first. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
